1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,156
They had just deleted a system for remembering too much; the river did not care what they had learned.

2
00:00:06,156 --> 00:00:14,790
The eighth arc began with a slight shake in the base of the field station, and within an hour the river was pouring over the road.

3
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:21,600
“We need to define the flood level, not merely to desire it,” the director said.

4
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:31,594
Almost every gauge was reading a number that was nearly the same as the last, and none of them was consistent with the water in the yard.

5
00:00:31,594 --> 00:00:38,433
A substantial part of the old model was built on a photographic memory of ten dry years.

6
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:44,383
“The yield is not proportional to the rain,” a leading hydrologist said.

7
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:53,537
“The main product of a bad model is suspicion.” He watched the water with a grin that was almost fearful.

8
00:00:53,537 --> 00:01:05,851
The unique thing about the district was its aptitude for holding water: ample ponds, a sizable canal, and a spontaneous habit of keeping the ditches clear.

9
00:01:05,851 --> 00:01:09,816
“You cannot eliminate a flood,” he said.

10
00:01:09,816 --> 00:01:22,145
“You can irrigate a field with it.” A weird, dreamy light on the water made the whole scene feel illusory, and the team could not render it in numbers.

11
00:01:22,145 --> 00:01:36,163
While the engineers sweated over the diagnosis, a legendary boatman diagnosed the problem in one sentence: the river was capable of a third channel, and the district had withdrawn from it.

12
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:47,282
“Incidentally, that is a rendering of the same story my grandfather told,” he said, and he was thirsty for someone to believe him.

13
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:52,322
The report was shaky and the agreement was agreeable only in name.

14
00:01:52,322 --> 00:01:57,641
“Add the local readings and cut the modelled ones,” Maya said.

15
00:01:57,641 --> 00:02:02,771
“The implicit assumption is that the river is incidental to our data.

16
00:02:02,771 --> 00:02:15,230
It is not.” Approximately a hundred people had signed, and they could agree on one point: their accounts were more consistent than the instruments.

17
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:29,524
Down at the field station, the team began to pass the old model through a smaller filter: even a ten per cent error was too much when a full street was under water, and less would have to mean more.

18
00:02:29,524 --> 00:02:35,253
The director left the door open for the way back, and nobody closed it.

19
00:02:35,253 --> 00:02:44,546
The term was significant: a similar event had happened once before, and nobody had written it down.
